I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: “Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate… The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” The two reply texts are, in full, the string "```json\n{\"a\":1}\n```\n```json\n{\"b\":2} {\"c\":3}\n```" and the string "```json\n{\"a\":1}\n```". For the first text, the rule requires the answer to be the object {"c":3}, because the second code fence holds two top-level objects and the last one inside that fence is {"c":3}. For the second text, the rule requires {"a":1}. The check recover_json_object returns {"a":1} for both: it collects fenced contents, tries them in reverse order, finds the second fenced content "{\"b\":2} {\"c\":3}" does not parse as a single dict, skips it, and returns the first fenced content {"a":1}. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if recover_json_object on the first text actually returns {"c":3} or raises, or if the rule is read as treating the entire content of a code fence as the candidate rather than the individual top-level objects inside it, in which case the first text’s required answer would also be {"a":1}.